::GEO_U04_L06_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio defines sine?
{
~A. adjacent/hypotenuse#This defines cosine.
=B. opposite/hypotenuse#Sine compares the opposite side to the hypotenuse.
~C. opposite/adjacent#This defines tangent.
~D. hypotenuse/opposite#This reverses sine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio defines cosine?
{
~A. opposite/hypotenuse#This defines sine.
~B. opposite/adjacent#This defines tangent.
~C. hypotenuse/adjacent#This reverses cosine.
=D. adjacent/hypotenuse#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio defines tangent?
{
=A. opposite/adjacent#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~B. opposite/hypotenuse#This defines sine.
~C. adjacent/hypotenuse#This defines cosine.
~D. hypotenuse/opposite#This uses the wrong denominator for tangent.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle A, opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 13. What is sin A?
{
~A. 12/13#This would use adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. 5/12#This would define tangent if 12 were adjacent.
=C. 5/13#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~D. 13/5#This reverses sine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle B, adjacent = 8 and hypotenuse = 17. What is cos B?
{
~A. 15/17#This would be sine if 15 were opposite.
=B. 8/17#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~C. 15/8#This would be tangent with opposite over adjacent.
~D. 17/8#This reverses cosine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle C, opposite = 7 and adjacent = 24. What is tan C?
{
=A. 7/24#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
~B. 24/7#This reverses tangent.
~C. 7/25#This would be sine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
~D. 24/25#This would be cosine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Why must the reference angle be identified?
{
~A. The hypotenuse changes with the reference angle.#The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle.
~B. The triangle stops being right.#The reference angle does not change the triangle type.
~C. The right angle becomes acute.#The right angle remains 90°.
=D. Opposite and adjacent depend on the chosen acute angle.#The hypotenuse stays fixed, but opposite and adjacent change with the reference angle.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio uses the hypotenuse and opposite side?
{
~A. Cosine#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~B. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
=C. Sine#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~D. Slope#Slope is not one of the three trig ratios here.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio uses the adjacent side and hypotenuse?
{
=A. Cosine#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~C. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~D. Perimeter#Perimeter is a sum of side lengths, not a trig ratio.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which ratio does not use the hypotenuse?
{
~A. Sine#Sine uses the hypotenuse.
~B. Cosine#Cosine uses the hypotenuse.
~C. Both sine and cosine#Both sine and cosine include the hypotenuse.
=D. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
SOH means which ratio?
{
~A. cos \= opposite/hypotenuse#Cosine is represented by CAH.
=B. sin \= opposite/hypotenuse#SOH stands for sine, opposite, hypotenuse.
~C. tan \= opposite/hypotenuse#Tangent is represented by TOA.
~D. sin \= adjacent/hypotenuse#That is cosine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
CAH means which ratio?
{
~A. sin \= adjacent/hypotenuse#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~B. tan \= adjacent/hypotenuse#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
=C. cos \= adjacent/hypotenuse#CAH stands for cosine, adjacent, hypotenuse.
~D. cos \= hypotenuse/adjacent#This reverses cosine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
TOA means which ratio?
{
~A. sin \= opposite/adjacent#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~B. cos \= opposite/adjacent#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~C. tan \= adjacent/opposite#This reverses tangent.
=D. tan \= opposite/adjacent#TOA stands for tangent, opposite, adjacent.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle D, opposite = 9 and hypotenuse = 41. What is sin D?
{
=A. 9/41#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~B. 40/41#This would be cosine if 40 is adjacent.
~C. 9/40#This would be tangent if 40 is adjacent.
~D. 41/9#This reverses sine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle E, adjacent = 20 and hypotenuse = 29. What is cos E?
{
~A. 21/29#This would use the opposite side if 21 is opposite.
~B. 21/20#This would be tangent with opposite over adjacent.
=C. 20/29#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~D. 29/20#This reverses cosine.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Relative to angle F, opposite = 11 and adjacent = 60. What is tan F?
{
~A. 60/11#This reverses tangent.
=B. 11/60#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
~C. 11/61#This would use the hypotenuse and match sine.
~D. 60/61#This would match cosine if 61 is the hypotenuse.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
A student writes cos A = opposite over hypotenuse. What correction is needed?
{
~A. Use opposite over hypotenuse for cosine.#That repeats the sine ratio.
~B. Use opposite over adjacent for cosine.#That defines tangent.
=C. Use adjacent over hypotenuse for cosine.#Cosine is defined as adjacent over hypotenuse.
~D. Use hypotenuse over adjacent for cosine.#This reverses the cosine ratio.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
A student writes tan A = adjacent over opposite. What correction is needed?
{
=A. Use opposite over adjacent.#Tangent is defined as opposite over adjacent.
~B. Use adjacent over hypotenuse.#That defines cosine.
~C. Use opposite over hypotenuse.#That defines sine.
~D. Use hypotenuse over opposite.#That is not tangent.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which side is opposite an acute reference angle?
{
~A. The side touching the reference angle and not the hypotenuse#That describes the adjacent side.
~B. The side across from the right angle#That describes the hypotenuse.
~C. The longest leg#Opposite depends on position relative to the reference angle.
=D. The side across from the reference angle#Opposite means across from the chosen angle.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which side is adjacent to an acute reference angle?
{
~A. The side across from the angle#That describes the opposite side.
=B. The leg touching the angle#Adjacent is the leg that touches the reference angle.
~C. The hypotenuse#The hypotenuse touches acute angles but is not called the adjacent leg in trig ratios.
~D. The side across from the right angle#That is the hypotenuse.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which first step best supports this lesson skill?
{
~A. Guess from the picture before checking labels.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. Identify the givens and the geometric object before choosing a relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Trigonometric Ratios.
~C. Skip directly to the final answer.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. Use a formula that is not connected to the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which explanation best shows mastery of this lesson?
{
~A. The answer only states a number.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. The answer says the diagram looks correct.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. The answer copies vocabulary without using it.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=D. The answer names the evidence, applies the correct relationship, and interprets the conclusion.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Trigonometric Ratios.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
What should a student check before submitting work for this lesson?
{
=A. That labels, notation, units, and reasoning match the lesson relationship.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Trigonometric Ratios.
~B. That every answer is longer than the choices.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~C. That no diagram or evidence is included.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. That the work uses a future unit topic.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which error should be avoided in this lesson?
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~B. Explaining why a theorem applies.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=C. Using appearance or an unrelated formula instead of the evidence in the task.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Trigonometric Ratios.
~D. Checking the final answer in context.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
::GEO_U04_L06_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.4
Question:
Which response is most aligned to the lesson objective?
{
~A. A response using a topic from a later unit.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
=B. A step-by-step solution for Trigonometric Ratios that cites the correct evidence.#This is correct because it begins with the lesson evidence and connects the work to Trigonometric Ratios.
~C. A response with no labels or explanation.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
~D. A response that changes the given information.#This does not show mastery because it omits the evidence, relationship, or interpretation needed for this lesson.
}
